content.text:
Existing save still has map boss col 1 and edges [1]. For current session test, we can reload/start a new run, but this illustrates save compatibility issue: existing saved runs generated before the fix will still be broken. Since game is new and only our test save, not a big deal. But if we want migration robustness, availableNodes can clamp/fallback: if last row and edges lead beyond length, normalize. Or mapScreen could use nodeKey(r, c) and boss col 1 won't match available. Better add migration in loadSavedGame or normalize map. Simpler: make availableNodes tolerant: for last row, return keys for actual array indices of next row if no valid edges, particularly boss. And enterNode uses nodeKey(row,col) requiring "8-0" for index 0; availableNodes returning 8-0 fixes. Let's update availableNodes:

```js
const nextRow = s.map.rows[r+1];
if (!nextRow) return [];
const keys = (node.edges||[]).filter(c => nextRow[c]).map(c=>nodeKey(r+1,c));
if (!keys.length && nextRow.length) return nextRow.map((_,c)=>nodeKey(r+1,c));
```

But for old save, edges [1], nextRow length 1 -> filter c=1 => nextRow[1] undefined -> keys []; fallback returns 8-0. Good. For normal nodes with no valid edges? Fallback all next row nodes could be overly permissive but better than dead end. Also could normalize boss col. Do this.

Additionally mapScreen keys are based on array index, consistent with enterNode row/col. Good. Apply.

input.newString:
const node = s.map.rows[r]?.[c];
if (!node) return [];
const nextRow = s.map.rows[r + 1] || [];
const keys = (node.edges || [])
  .filter(col => nextRow[col])
  .map(col => nodeKey(r + 1, col));
if (keys.length) return keys;
// Safety net for old saves or malformed edges (notably the single boss row).
return nextRow.map((_, col) => nodeKey(r + 1, col));

